WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.599 --> 00:00:05.410
Mainstream TV viewers often overlook horror,
but American Horror Story managed to seduce

00:00:05.410 --> 00:00:06.880
an audience all its own.

00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:11.240
Here's a look behind the scenes into what
it took to bring the beloved anthology series

00:00:11.240 --> 00:00:12.260
to the small screen.

00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:18.680
Before American Horror Story debuted in 2011,
co-creator Ryan Murphy was best known as one

00:00:18.680 --> 00:00:23.369
of the minds behind Glee, a show so happy
and positive that it was literally about a

00:00:23.369 --> 00:00:28.360
bunch of high school kids gaining self esteem
by singing and dancing to familiar pop songs.

00:00:28.400 --> 00:00:33.000
Murphy then went directly from his high school
musical to a house full of ghosts, violence,

00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:33.960
and a Rubber Man.

00:00:34.249 --> 00:00:35.240
What happened?

00:00:35.240 --> 00:00:36.060
Who hurt you, Ryan?

00:00:36.249 --> 00:00:40.559
In an interview with Elle, Murphy admitted:
"I was like, 'I can't write any more nice

00:00:40.559 --> 00:00:44.129
speeches for these Glee kids about love and
tolerance and togetherness.'"

00:00:44.129 --> 00:00:47.680
To that end, he wrote a show about horror
and depravity instead.

00:00:47.760 --> 00:00:51.340
American Horror Story also allowed Murphy
to right a Glee wrong.

00:00:51.359 --> 00:00:56.559
He'd wanted Sarah Paulson to play Emma Pillsbury,
the high school guidance counselor eventually

00:00:56.560 --> 00:00:58.260
portrayed by Jayma Mays.

00:00:58.440 --> 00:01:02.100
Paulson was unable because she was starring
in a Broadway play at the time.

00:01:02.100 --> 00:01:07.140
Murphy finally found a place for Paulson as
medium Billie Dean Howard, the first of many

00:01:07.150 --> 00:01:10.280
memorable AHS roles she would step into.

00:01:10.280 --> 00:01:11.580
"So much pain here.

00:01:16.040 --> 00:01:18.940
So much longing and regret."

00:01:20.040 --> 00:01:24.640
While occasionally campy and darkly funny,
most every season of American Horror Story

00:01:24.649 --> 00:01:26.510
gets pretty bleak pretty quickly.

00:01:26.510 --> 00:01:30.340
"I mean, just because you've got 30 bodies
buried in your crawl space, don't mean you

00:01:30.340 --> 00:01:34.799
can't have a really terrific rec room and
be a respectable businessman."

00:01:34.799 --> 00:01:38.990
Playing murderers, and their victims, can
really get under the skin of the actors, so

00:01:38.990 --> 00:01:41.520
a few have tried to relieve the tension when
possible.

00:01:41.690 --> 00:01:45.660
Zachary Quinto took on a major role in Asylum
as Oliver Thredson.

00:01:45.679 --> 00:01:51.080
Another role he accepted: on-set music therapist,
playing his banjo on set to lighten the mood.

00:01:51.080 --> 00:01:55.490
Lily Rabe plays guitar in real life, and she'd
play to provide "musical breaks" on tough

00:01:55.490 --> 00:01:56.380
days too.

00:01:56.740 --> 00:01:57.240
"Yeah.

00:01:57.240 --> 00:01:57.940
Musical breaks.

00:01:58.080 --> 00:01:59.800
It was good.

00:01:59.900 --> 00:02:03.940
Zach is better at the banjo than I am at the
guitar, for sure."

00:02:04.660 --> 00:02:09.520
Jessica Lange, the grande dame of American
Horror Story, convinced Ryan Murphy to put

00:02:09.520 --> 00:02:12.200
one of these melodic palette cleansers into
the show itself.

00:02:12.280 --> 00:02:16.680
After a flashback revealed that her Asylum
character had once been a singer in a band,

00:02:16.680 --> 00:02:22.000
Lange suggested a full-on fantasy musical
sequence in the middle of a psychiatric institution,

00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:27.230
set to the classic '60s novelty song "The
Name Game," if only because, as she told Vulture:

00:02:27.230 --> 00:02:28.900
"Everything we do is so grim."

00:02:29.900 --> 00:02:32.540
Let's be real: Lots of people are afraid of
clowns.

00:02:32.540 --> 00:02:37.540
American Horror Story aptly exploited those
fears in its Freak Show season, featuring

00:02:37.540 --> 00:02:41.800
John Carroll Lynch as the monstrous Twisty
the Clown, who isn't so much a children's

00:02:41.800 --> 00:02:46.660
party entertainer as he is a guy who kills
people with scissors and locks kids in an

00:02:46.660 --> 00:02:47.580
old school bus.

00:02:47.960 --> 00:02:55.340
"Oh clown, you're so talented, please release
us so we can tell the world."

00:02:55.980 --> 00:02:59.680
Perhaps unsurprisingly, this subplot upset
some real-life clowns.

00:02:59.740 --> 00:03:05.180
In 2014, Clowns of America president Glenn
Kohlberger said in response to Freak Show:

00:03:05.190 --> 00:03:08.040
"Hollywood makes money sensationalizing the
norm.

00:03:08.040 --> 00:03:12.700
They can take any situation no matter how
good or pure and turn it into a nightmare.

00:03:12.700 --> 00:03:18.320
We do not support in any way, shape, or form
any medium that sensationalizes or adds to

00:03:18.320 --> 00:03:19.000
coulrophobia."

00:03:19.120 --> 00:03:24.860
The protests flared up again in 2017, when
American Horror Story: Cult once again featured

00:03:24.860 --> 00:03:25.900
evil clowns.

00:03:25.940 --> 00:03:30.480
World Clown Association president Pam Moody
told the Detroit Free Press:

00:03:30.480 --> 00:03:34.820
"The role of a clown persona, regardless of
their style of clowning, is meant to bring

00:03:34.820 --> 00:03:37.660
joy and laughter, to bring humor to a hurting
world."

00:03:38.700 --> 00:03:44.080
Before it was canceled in 2017, Scream Queens,
Ryan Murphy's other other show, was the place

00:03:44.090 --> 00:03:47.890
where the blood and gore of American Horror
Story met the comedy of Glee.

00:03:47.890 --> 00:03:52.970
For instance, its first season asked the burning
question: How might a cast made up of self-absorbed

00:03:52.970 --> 00:03:58.310
sorority girls and dumb frat guys deal with
a costumed serial killer roaming their college

00:03:58.310 --> 00:03:58.800
campus?

00:03:58.980 --> 00:04:00.420
"Good evening, idiot hookers.

00:04:00.420 --> 00:04:04.520
I'm very happy to welcome you to hell week
here at Kappa House."

00:04:04.810 --> 00:04:09.460
It was also laced with references to classic
slasher movies, tongue-in-cheek horror movie

00:04:09.470 --> 00:04:13.310
tropes, and it even starred Halloween's Jamie
Lee Curtis in a major role.

00:04:13.310 --> 00:04:17.440
While that may all sound pretty standard for
the guy who conjured up the Dead Breakfast

00:04:17.440 --> 00:04:21.880
Club, Scream Queens nearly had even more in
common with American Horror Story.

00:04:22.040 --> 00:04:23.180
Just how much, you ask?

00:04:23.340 --> 00:04:28.480
Well, it was almost a spin-off of Coven, the
AHS season set at a school for young witches

00:04:28.480 --> 00:04:29.480
in New Orleans.

00:04:29.780 --> 00:04:31.200
"Surprise, bitch.

00:04:31.420 --> 00:04:33.260
I bet you thought you'd seen the last of me."

00:04:33.280 --> 00:04:37.820
However, Murphy changed his mind after a lunch
with Fox executives, explaining:

00:04:37.830 --> 00:04:42.030
"We were talking about things that we loved
that were not on the air, things that we loved

00:04:42.030 --> 00:04:44.390
growing up, movies that we loved watching."

00:04:44.390 --> 00:04:48.970
Once the subject of slasher movies came up,
Scream Queens' own unique identity began to

00:04:48.970 --> 00:04:53.440
take shape, and while its ride was a short
one, it sure was a blast while it lasted.

00:05:03.820 --> 00:05:09.210
In the months before the sixth season of American
Horror Story premiered in 2016, FX released

00:05:09.210 --> 00:05:12.900
more than 20 teasers, each suggesting a totally
different theme.

00:05:13.040 --> 00:05:18.820
At a Television Critics Association event,
FX CEO John Landgraf explained that only one

00:05:18.820 --> 00:05:22.980
of them accurately represented the season,
while the rest were "misdirects."

00:05:23.380 --> 00:05:27.900
Though wildly different, the trailers did
share a few things in common: all of them

00:05:27.900 --> 00:05:32.332
were creepy, weird, and pretty damn unsettling,
perhaps none more so than one that showed

00:05:32.332 --> 00:05:37.500
a woman using some hedge clippers to snip
wind chimes made from human teeth.

00:05:37.500 --> 00:05:42.080
Turns out that was the real trailer, as that
imagery figures into American Horror Story:

00:05:42.080 --> 00:05:42.740
Roanoke.

00:05:42.980 --> 00:05:45.800
In a way, though, all of the fake trailers
were "real."

00:05:45.800 --> 00:05:50.390
That season of the show explored the notions
of "real" and "fake," as the plot centered

00:05:50.390 --> 00:05:54.970
in part on a couple who lived in a haunted
house near the lost colony of Roanoke, and

00:05:54.970 --> 00:05:59.669
a TV show's re-creations of the events they
endured at the hands of the ghostly Roanoke

00:05:59.669 --> 00:06:00.660
colonists.

00:06:01.340 --> 00:06:06.300
In American Horror Story: Hotel, Evan Peters
portrayed Mr. March, who turned out to be

00:06:06.300 --> 00:06:11.640
arguably the most super evil character in
the show's long list of super evil characters.

00:06:11.860 --> 00:06:17.060
"I look around, and I see the definition of
American success."

00:06:17.300 --> 00:06:17.800
Why?

00:06:17.800 --> 00:06:22.300
Well, because he trapped, tortured, and killed
people in the Hotel Cortez, duh.

00:06:22.300 --> 00:06:26.820
It's quite upsetting then that, according
to Ryan Murphy in Entertainment Weekly, Mr.

00:06:26.830 --> 00:06:28.740
March is based on a real person.

00:06:28.740 --> 00:06:35.040
That individual is H.H. Holmes, who, during
the Chicago World's Fair in 1893, opened and

00:06:35.040 --> 00:06:36.400
ran a "hotel of horrors."

00:06:36.660 --> 00:06:41.400
Visitors to the great exposition who tragically
chose to stay at Holmes' hotel would find

00:06:41.400 --> 00:06:45.750
their rooms soundproof and windowless, all
the better for Holmes to take them out via

00:06:45.750 --> 00:06:47.260
gassing or other means.

00:06:47.420 --> 00:06:51.920
Secret passageways allowed him to remove bodies
to the basement without detection, where he'd

00:06:51.930 --> 00:06:53.580
destroy the evidence in a furnace.

00:06:53.580 --> 00:06:57.650
He was caught in 1894, and executed two years
later.

